# OPENPGPKEY (OpenPGP Public Key) Records
# ===============================================
# OPENPGPKEY records store OpenPGP public keys in DNS for verification,
# allowing email clients and other tools to retrieve and verify PGP keys.
#
# Format: OPENPGPKEY <base64-encoded-key>
# The key is stored in a specific binary format and then base64-encoded.
#
# For Trigger service PGP key:
#   Primary key: mQINBF23456gBEADZ3JzIDxzZXJ2ZXJAZXhhbXBsZS5jb20+INh1
#   (This is a placeholder - replace with actual key during deployment)
#
# Generate OPENPGPKEY from a PGP key:
#   1. Export key in the required binary format
#   2. Base64 encode it
#   gpg --export-options export-reset-subkey-passwd --export --armor key-id | ...
#
# To generate the binary representation for DNS:
#   gpg --export key-id | openpgp2dns | base64 -w0
#
# Or manually create from ASCII-armored key:
#   1. Remove headers/footers and newlines
#   2. Base64 decode
#   3. Prepend with 0x00 0x04 0x00 (for OpenPGP key format)
#   4. Base64 encode the result
#
# NOTE: Authoritative OPENPGPKEY record is defined in security/bind9/trigger.zone
# This file is provided for web servers that serve .well-known/ for documentation.
#
# For more information: RFC 7929 (OPENPGPKEY)
# https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7929
#
# For key generation and management, see:
# https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc4880.txt (OpenPGP Message Format)
# https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-kocher-opendnssec-openpgpkey-00
